WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
- The world needs more close friendships

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.320
that maybe don't necessarily
look like other friendships.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.570
So, what do you think
is important to remember

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:10.010
as we all start these conversations?

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.640
Because, and I say start
these conversations,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.620
but they've been happening
for years and generations,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.290
but what do you think
the most important thing,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.930
like when people see y'all
out, that's a normal thing.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.830
It's normal, but for some reason

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.540
it's not for a lot of people.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.300
And I think that's why
everything is going on,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.290
You know, Black Lives Matter,

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:28.770
and all these protests and everything.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.852
What do you think the
conversation should be?

00:00:31.852 --> 00:00:33.140
- Wow, go ahead Zach.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.080
- Well, I'll just go first
and say I did grow up,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.310
I grew up in New Jersey,
and New York, and LA.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.720
So I've always grown up
in very diverse places,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.153
and it was never,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.520
having African American friends,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.310
and Hispanic friends, and Asian friends,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.509
I grew up in an area where I thought

00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:56.490
there were just as many Jewish
people as Christian people

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.830
because I grew up in an area

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.510
where there were so many Jewish people.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.510
So I think that that did give
me a leg up in a sense of

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.500
loving and being around people

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.110
of all different types.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.509
That having been said, I
think this conversation is new

00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:16.660
for me in a sense, because I'm now going,

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.860
as I'm listening to everyone
who's speaking and reading,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.900
I know that I too have lots to learn.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.010
It's not enough to say, "Oh,
my best friend is a black man."

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.557
It's not enough to say,

00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:31.577
"Oh, I grew up with a diverse population."

00:01:31.577 --> 00:01:33.570
There's ways that I can be

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:34.950
way more proactive than I'm being.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.547
I can't just be, just
sit back and go, "Oh, no,

00:01:37.547 --> 00:01:39.750
"I'm cool, I have a black best friend."

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.680
That's B.S.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.000
There's plenty of ways that I haven't been

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.100
being proactive in my own
life, and my own privilege.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.910
And I'm really learning that now.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:49.982
I'm taking this opportunity

00:01:49.982 --> 00:01:52.190
to really listen, and to really learn,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.170
and see how I can improve
my way of being as well.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.460
- When you're a kid,
you don't look at color.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.400
Your friends are your
friends, you know what I mean?

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.620
And it's not until your
parents get into your head,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.920
or outside influences get into your head,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.983
and tell you that people
are different than you are.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:17.440
So I encourage everyone
who is not a minority,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.380
or not a person of color,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.770
to really learn about people of color.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.943
And by listening, and doing your research.

00:02:28.119 --> 00:02:29.393
It's very, very,

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.420
It's very, very scary to

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.390
be judged by the color of your skin.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.720
It's happened to me quite
a bit my whole life,

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.890
you know what I mean?

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.860
Even how people describe me

00:02:44.951 --> 00:02:46.600
on "Scrubs".

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.230
I'm the black guy from
"Scrubs," you know what I mean?

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.630
I'm not even one of the
actors from "Scrubs,"

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.500
I'm the black guy from "Scrubs".

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.750
And so, I encourage everyone to just,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.809
first of all, educate yourself.

00:02:58.809 --> 00:02:59.660
- Yeah.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.980
- That's most important,
you know what I mean?

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.990
Once you educate yourself,
you'll be able to

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.350
see that everyone,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.160
pretty much, is the same.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.750
We might do things differently,
but we all are the same,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.380
and together we can abolish racism.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.170
And it's everywhere.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.660
And also, once you recognize that,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.050
once you recognize racism is
everywhere, it never left.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.340
It's always been here,
you know what I mean?

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.020
It's not a surprise that
this is going on right now.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.010
It's finally coming to a
head is what's happening.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:35.843
- Yeah.

00:03:35.843 --> 00:03:37.373
- But it's been here forever,

00:03:38.732 --> 00:03:41.130
and once you don't take that for granted,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.610
and you recognize that
there's racism out there,

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.150
you'll start checking yourself.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.290
You'll start checking
yourself, believe it or not.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.170
- It's interesting.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.780
People are so afraid to talk to our kids,

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:50.960
but those kids are the ones

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.970
that we're helping shape
and mold with those ideals,

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.020
and those conversations, I think,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.560
are just as important to
have with your five-year-old

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.620
that it is to have with your mom.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.610
You know what I'm saying?

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.160
But it's so good.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.800
Thank you guys for being
open to talking about it.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.220
I know it's not a comfortable
position for a lot of people,

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.180
but sometimes it's good
to get uncomfortable,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.150
because I don't feel like
we're gonna progress.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.520
I think we're gonna
keep repeating the cycle

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.561
unless we keep talking about it.

00:04:16.561 --> 00:04:17.600
- Yeah, it's all for not

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.721
if we don't continue the conversation.

00:04:19.721 --> 00:04:20.554
- Yeah.

00:04:20.554 --> 00:04:21.387
- And the conversation needs to continue,

00:04:21.387 --> 00:04:22.220
you know what I mean?

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.310
It's not just about police brutality.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.810
It's also about equal rights.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.670
It's about everyone being
able to live in America

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.222
the way everyone else
does, you know what I mean?

00:04:32.222 --> 00:04:33.160
- Yeah!

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.267
- I should be able to, you said,

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:37.250
"Walking into a store and
getting your bag checked."

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.880
That doesn't even phase me anymore

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.873
because I'm used to it, whereas--

00:04:40.873 --> 00:04:41.706
- No, that's what I'm saying.

00:04:41.706 --> 00:04:43.267
My friend brought that up,
and I was like, "What?"

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:44.530
"When did that happen?"

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.610
We're at a mall on tour,
and I'm just like, "What?"

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.953
And she's crying 'cause
she's just, she's exhausted.

00:04:49.953 --> 00:04:50.793
It's exhausting!

00:04:50.793 --> 00:04:53.630
- It is exhausting, but we're at the point

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.670
where the conversation
is being talked about,

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:56.860
and more than usual.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.262
More than it used to be,
and I am very appreciative

00:05:01.262 --> 00:05:05.420
for everyone that's willing
to learn and listen.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.700
I'm very appreciative for
that, you know what I mean?

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.690
I'm glad that we are all

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.032
taking steps in the right direction.

00:05:12.032 --> 00:05:12.865
- Yeah, no, can't.
- We still have

00:05:12.865 --> 00:05:13.698
a long way to go,

00:05:13.698 --> 00:05:14.580
but we're taking steps
in the right direction.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.300
- Yeah, you can't grow
unless you're learning.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.843
So I'm with you there.

